    <string name="prep_tip_yams_1">Aim for a strong upper-section row early to secure the bonus.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_yams_2">When a roll is average, keep a flexible pair to chase a full house or three of a kind next turn.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_yams_3">Do not force Yams too early: maximize stable points first.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_yams_4">Use chance as a safety net when no strong combination appears.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_yams_5">Once the upper bonus is on track, focus on high-value boxes (full house, straights, Yams).</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_yahtzee_1">Prioritize regular combinations early to limit zeroes.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_yahtzee_2">A weak four of a kind can score less than a straight: compare before validating.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_yahtzee_3">Keep a plan B on every roll to avoid blocking a key row.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_yahtzee_4">Secure easy combinations first to keep choices open late in the game.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_yahtzee_5">When two options are close, choose the one that keeps the next roll most flexible.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_custom_1">Start with fixed-value rules to secure a base of points.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_custom_2">If a custom rule is risky, wait for a naturally favorable roll.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_custom_3">Spread risks between players to keep the game close.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_custom_4">Spot the most profitable custom rules and save them for truly favorable rolls.</string>
    <string name="prep_tip_custom_5">When a rule is rarely achievable, do not hesitate to sacrifice it early to manage the rest.</string>
